---
layout: article
title: Events
description: Events are the records every Appwrite Analytics metric is built from. Learn which events the tracking helpers send, how custom events differ, and what Appwrite adds to each event.
---

An event is one thing that happened in your app: a page was viewed, a link was clicked, a purchase completed. Every figure on an analytics dashboard, from visitors to bounce rate, is calculated from the events a property receives.

Each event has a name and the URL it happened on. It can also carry a referrer, the viewport width, scroll depth, engagement time, and custom props.

# Automatic events {% #automatic %}

The tracking helpers in the Appwrite SDKs send these events for you. "Mobile" means the Android, Apple, Flutter, and React Native SDKs.

| Event | Sent by | When |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pageview` | Web | A page loads or the route changes |
| `outbound_link` | Web | A visitor clicks a link to another site |
| `file_download` | Web | A visitor clicks a link to a file, once download tracking is on |
| `scroll_depth` | Web | The visitor leaves a page or changes route after scrolling, with how far they scrolled |
| `engagement_time` | Web | The tab is hidden, the route changes, or the page unloads, with the seconds the page was visible |
| `screen_view` | Mobile | A screen is shown, from your navigation or a `screenView()` call |
| `first_open` | Mobile | The app starts for the first time after it's installed |
| `app_updated` | Mobile | The app starts for the first time after an update, with `previousVersion` and `version` |
| `app_backgrounded` | Mobile | The app moves to the background, with the seconds it was in the foreground |
| `app_foregrounded` | Mobile | The app returns to the foreground |
| `push_received` | Mobile | Your code calls `pushReceived()` when a push notification arrives |
| `push_opened` | Mobile | Your code calls `pushOpened()` when someone taps a push notification |

`push_received` is billed like any event, but it never counts toward visitors or visits, because a push can arrive while the app is closed.

For each helper's options, see [Track a website](/docs/products/analytics/web) and [Track a mobile app](/docs/products/analytics/mobile).

# Custom events {% #custom %}

Any event with a name of your own is a custom event, such as `signup_completed` or `invite_sent`. The **Custom events** card on the dashboard lists them, separate from the automatic events. See [Track custom events](/docs/products/analytics/custom-events).

`pageview` and `screen_view` are the two names Appwrite treats differently. Both count as views: they count toward **Pageviews** and **Views per visit**, and set each visit's entry and exit pages. Every other event counts toward visitors, visits, and events, but not pageviews. A custom event also counts as an interaction that ends a bounce, unless it's sent with `interactive: false`. See [What a visit records](/docs/products/analytics/visitors#visit-metrics).

# Where events come from {% #sources %}

- **A website**: the Web SDK's `Tracking` helper sends events from the browser. On Appwrite Sites, turning on analytics adds it with no code. See [Track a website](/docs/products/analytics/web).
- **A mobile app**: the `Tracking` helper in the Android, Apple, Flutter, and React Native SDKs sends events from the app. See [Track a mobile app](/docs/products/analytics/mobile).
- **Any other client**: sends one HTTP request per event. See [Send events over REST](/docs/products/analytics/rest).
- **Your server**: sends events with an API key, on behalf of a visitor. See [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side).

Events from browsers and apps need no API key. Appwrite accepts one only when its origin passes two checks: it matches a Web app's hostname or a mobile app's package name or bundle ID under **Apps** in your project, and the property's [allowed origins](/docs/products/analytics/properties#allowed-origins) include it.

# What Appwrite adds {% #enrichment %}

When an event arrives, Appwrite adds details it works out from the request and the event's fields:

- **Page**: the path and hostname of the URL, without the query string and fragment.
- **Traffic source**: the referring site, the channel, and UTM parameters. See [Traffic sources](/docs/products/analytics/sources).
- **Location**: country, region, and city, from the IP address.
- **Technology**: browser, operating system, and device from the user agent, and a screen size category from the viewport width.
- **Bot details**: whether a bot sent the event, and which one. See [Bots and AI traffic](/docs/products/analytics/bots).
- **Visitor and visit**: which visitor sent the event, and which visit it belongs to. See [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).
- **User**: the signed-in user's ID, when the property's **Identify signed-in users** setting is on. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties#user-identification).

Events usually appear on the dashboard within a few seconds.
